<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Callixtus III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1378">1378</a>–<a href="/years/1458">1458</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Alfons de Borja · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-borgia">House of Borgia</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Callixtus III, born Alfons de Borja near Xàtiva in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, was pope from 1455 to 1458, the first of the Borgia popes. Formerly an adviser to Alfonso V of Aragon, he devoted his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and ordered the noon bell for prayers during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1456. He also had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>'s condemnation annulled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii.jpg" alt="Pope Callixtus III" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sano di Pietro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calixtus_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Borgia was born in 1378 at La Torreta in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, then part of the Crown of Aragon, the eldest child of Juan Domingo de Borja y Doncel and Francina Llançol. His sister Isabel became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>. He studied grammar, logic and the arts in Valencia and in 1392 went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, where he obtained a doctorate in both canon law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> and began his career as a professor of law. Around 1411 he attended a sermon by Vincent Ferrer, whom he later canonised on 3 June 1455.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Borgia was chosen in 1416 as delegate of the Diocese of Lerida to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, but King Alfonso V of Aragon opposed the council and he did not take part. From 1424 he served the Aragonese king, who wished to see him a cardinal; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> refused. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Great Western Schism</a> he supported Antipope Benedict XIII and was the driving force behind Antipope Clement VIII's submission to Martin V in 1429. Martin V then made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. He acted as a diplomat for the kings of Aragon, tutored Alfonso's illegitimate son Ferrante, and was made a cardinal by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> on 2 May 1444 after reconciling the pope and Alfonso.</p><p>He was elected pope at an advanced age as a compromise candidate in the conclave of 8 April 1455. Less than two years after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, he was chiefly concerned with organising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. A building programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was cancelled and its money turned to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and nuncios and preachers were sent across Europe, though the princes were slow to respond because of national rivalries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> defeated the Turks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 22 July 1456. On 29 June 1456 the pope had ordered church bells rung at noon to call the faithful to pray for the defenders, and he fixed the Feast of the Transfiguration on 6 August to mark the victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Callixtus ordered a new trial for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>, at which she was vindicated after her death. He canonised four saints and appointed nine cardinals in two consistories in 1456, among them his nephew Rodrigo de Borgia, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>. A story first found in a biography of 1475 holds that he excommunicated Halley's Comet; no known primary source supports it, and his bull of 29 June 1456 does not mention the comet. He died on 6 August 1458 and in his will left 5000 ducats to found a hospital. The Catholic historian Ludwig von Pastor judged that, except for his nepotism, he deserved high praise for his energy in defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> against Turkish power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Callixtus III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two popes, <b>Callixtus III</b> and Alexander VI of the Borgia family, came from the Kingdom of Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunyadi's defence of Belgrade in 1456 was celebrated throughout Europe, and the noon bell ordered by <b>Pope Callixtus III</b> to call for prayers for the Christian forces became associated with the victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A retrial authorised by <b>Pope Callixtus III</b> quashed Joan of Arc's 1431 conviction and declared her innocent.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Torreta de Canals, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.96,-0.59&amp;year=1430" aria-label="Torreta de Canals on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4535 767 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Callixtus III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 1037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 1037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.96,-0.59&amp;year=1430">What was here in 1430? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
